#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/id #uuid "3a18e6a6-e190-5977-9e1f-0d115b16c9fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5703" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overline{x}=15$ and variance $\\sigma^2=3.25$. Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=17.3$, giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{x-\\overline{x}}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=15\n$$\n\nand we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{3.25}\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=17.3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x - x^(―))/(σ) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{17.3 - 15}{\\sqrt{3.25}} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/id #uuid "1c03d476-51f5-5dc4-bed6-04986f318924" :question/math-academy-id "e-5705" :question/problem "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma=1.2$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=13.2$ and $z$-score $z=-1.5$. Find the mean of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=13.2$, $z=-1.5$, and $\\sigma=1.2$ into the formula and solving for $\\overline{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= x^(―) + z \\cdot σ \\\\\n13.2 &= x^(―) + (-1.5) \\cdot 1.2 \\\\\n13.2 &= x^(―) - 1.8 \\\\\nx^(―) &= 13.2 + 1.8 \\\\\nx^(―) &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "68d14643-0faf-5cff-b4b9-cdb903946c39" :question/math-academy-id "e-8235" :question/problem "The mean height of a group of basketball players is $178\\textrm{cm}$. A player in this group is $184\\textrm{cm}$ tall and his $z$-score is $z = 1.5$. Find the standard deviation of the basketball players' heights." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=184$,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=178\n$$\n\nand $z=1.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= x^(―) + z \\cdot σ \\\\\n184 &= 178 + 1.5 \\cdot σ \\\\\n6 &= 1.5 \\cdot σ \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{6}{1.5} \\\\\nσ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the players heights is $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083038 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/id #uuid "c4f84572-0883-5fb5-b5d8-513d1aa2f50a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8236" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations $x_1=18, x_2=12$ with $z$-scores $z_1=2.8$ and $z_2=1.6$, respectively. Find the mean of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= x^(¯) + 2.8 \\cdot σ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= x^(¯) + 1.6 \\cdot σ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first and solving for $\\sigma$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(18 - 12) &= (x^(¯) - x^(¯)) + (2.8 - 1.6) \\cdot σ \\\\\n6 &= 1.2 \\cdot σ \\\\\nσ &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\bar{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= x^(¯) + 2.8 \\cdot σ \\\\\n18 &= x^(¯) + 2.8 \\cdot 5 \\\\\nx^(¯) &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
